        letter: `I did not fall in love with you in a single moment. I want to be honest about that, because the movies lie and I do not want to lie to you. It was not one look across a room. It was a slow, accumulating certainty, like sunrise — you do not see the exact second night becomes day, but at some point you realise the darkness is gone and everything is lit.

But there was a day I *knew.* A day the slow thing became a certain thing.

It was a small moment, which is fitting, because you are made of small moments. You were doing something kind for someone who could not thank you properly and did not even fully notice. You were not performing it. You did not know I was paying attention. You were just being who you are when you think no one is watching — and that, I have learned, is the only version of a person that matters. Anyone can be good in front of an audience. You were good in the quiet. And watching you be good in the quiet, something in my chest simply settled, the way a key settles into a lock, and a voice inside me said, clearly and without drama: *this one. Her. This is the person.*

Love is not the feeling of butterflies. Butterflies are easy; anyone can give you butterflies. Love is watching how a person treats the world when there is nothing in it for them, and deciding you want to build a life beside that. Love is seeing someone's flaws up close — the real ones, the human ones — and finding that they do not diminish the person but somehow make them more real, more precious, more yours. That is what happened to me. I stopped loving an idea of you and started loving the actual you, with all your complexity, and the actual you was better than any idea I could have invented.

I knew I loved you because your happiness started to matter more to me than my own. That sounds like a line, but I mean it as a literal fact about my inner life. When something good happened to you, I felt it before I felt my own good news. When something hurt you, it hurt me in a place I did not know I had. Your peace became the thing I wanted to protect above almost everything. That is the clearest sign I have ever found: when someone else's wellbeing becomes load-bearing in your own heart.

I knew because I started imagining a future and you were simply in all of it. Not as a decision — as an assumption. Every version of the years ahead that felt like *home* had you in it, and every version without you felt like a life I would merely survive rather than live. You had become the default setting of my hope.

And I knew because of the way I wanted to become better. Real love does not make you comfortable with who you are; it makes you want to grow into who you could be. Loving you made me look honestly at myself — at my patience, my temper, my selfishness, my faith — and want to fix the parts that were not worthy of you. You did not ask me to change. You just made me want to be a man worth staying near.

I should have told you all of this when it mattered. That is one of my regrets, and I will not hide from it. But I am telling you now, as plainly as I know how: I know I love you. Not the fever kind that burns out. The quiet, permanent, chosen kind. The kind that survives silence. The kind that, given the chance, would spend the rest of its life proving itself.`
